The issue is the CFLAGS are doubled. Look:
```
-Wstrict-prototypes -Wno-attributes -Wno-unknown-pragmas -Wno-psabi -nostdlib -pipe -Wstrict-prototypes -Wno-attributes -Wno-unknown-pragmas -Wno-psabi -nostdlib -pipe
```

This is because the build is being done from the wrong directory (nuttx root instead of vendor_bes/boards/...) so the Make.defs gets included twice. But actually the real issue is that the CFLAGS are being accumulated twice, and this might be making the command too long or causing some other issue.

Wait, actually looking more carefully, when I ran `make -C nuttx` from the nuttx directory, the Board.mk's `.depend` target runs `$(MKDEP) $(DEPPATH) $(CC) -- $(CFLAGS) -- $(SRCS)`.

The `$(DEPPATH)` includes `--dep-path board --dep-path src` and that appears twice because of the include chain.

But the mkdeps error I got was "No compiler specified" when the CFLAGS had too many flags. When I simplified it, it worked.

Wait, let me re-read the mkdeps code more carefully. The issue might be with the number of `--` separators in the command. Looking at the command:

```
mkdeps --dep-path board --dep-path src --dep-path board --dep-path src arm-none-eabi-gcc -- CFLAGS -- FILES
```

There are TWO `--` separators. After the first `--`, g_cc gets set to g_cflags (which is NULL at that point since dep-path options don't go into g_cflags). Then after the second `--`, g_cc gets set to g_cflags (which is the stuff between the first and second `--`).

Wait, that's not right either. Let me re-read the code:

```c
for (argidx = 1; argidx < argc; argidx++)
{
  if (strcmp(argv[argidx], "--") == 0)
  {
    g_cc = g_cflags;
    g_cflags = args;
    args = NULL;
    group++;
  }
  else if (strcmp(argv[argidx], "--dep-path") == 0)
  {
    argidx++;
    if (args)
      append(&args, argv[argidx]);
    else
      append(&g_altpath, argv[argidx]);
  }
  else
  {
    append(&args, argv[argidx]);
  }
}
```

So:
- Before first `--`: `--dep-path` goes to g_altpath (since args is NULL). `arm-none-eabi-gcc` goes to `args`.
- At first `--`: `g_cc = g_cflags` (NULL), `g_cflags = args` ("arm-none-eabi-gcc"), `args = NULL`
- Between first and second `--`: CFLAGS accumulate in `args`
- At second `--`: `g_cc = g_cflags` ("arm-none-eabi-gcc"), `g_cflags = args` (CFLAGS), `args = NULL`
- After second `--`: FILES go to `args`

After loop, `g_files = args`.

So g_cc = "arm-none-eabi-gcc". This should work!

But wait - the test I ran manually DID work with the same flags. But when make runs it, it fails. Could it be that make is doing something different with the argument passing?

Actually, I notice that the `$(DEPPATH)` is duplicated: `--dep-path board --dep-path src --dep-path board --dep-path src`. That's4 dep-path args, not 2. But that shouldn't matter.

Wait, I think the issue might be that the Makefile runs the command inside a shell and the quoting might be different. Let me try running the exact command from the build output.

